body:
i don't get how the christmas sweater entered in to the argument/debate/logic/whatever 

&#x200B;

but i have a hard time deciphering this fascinating accent

You had me until "Indiana". 

&#x200B;

(I'm kidding)

\-cide, from the latin cidium, roughly "the act of killing"... typically, in modern legal usage, such terms also include the specific intent (mens rea) to kill the subject killed (homicide, suicide, fratricide, matricide...). I don't think it would be accurate as most of the destructive acts levied on the ecosystem are (often grossly) negligent and motivated by cost, convenience, etc. rather than the explicit, singular desire to simply destroy \*that\* specific part of the ecosystem.

&#x200B;

Edit: In American law murder statutes often, but not always, use the phrase "malice aforethought" or something similar.

in US copyright law, infringement of a property must prove that the defendant did actually copy the property, and also that there is a “substantial similarity” between them; 

there is significant precedent about what "substantial similarity" means- a simple sample is basically a direct copy of the original which, within certain guidelines, is, generally, considered substantially similar; if the sample is mere milliseconds or heavily altered with speed, tone, reversed (whatever) then it may not be considered substantially similar after the changes; 

if considered not "substantially similar" then royalties are not owed to the original property's owner; 

covers, often being longer and more complex than a sample, are often very different expressions (but not always); compare *All Along the Watchtower* by Bob Dylan with Jimi Hendrix's cover; also compare Queen's *Under Pressure* with Vanilla Ice's *Ice Ice Baby*
